   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Komunitas KAHE is a non-profit organization in Maumere, East Nusa Tenggara, Indonesia. Komunitas KAHE focuses its works on art and cultural activities as a form of creative civic engagement to create democratic and just civil society in Sikka regency in particular, and Flores in general. KAHE sees art and culture as a medium to produce knowledge integrated dialectically with the development of society. Therefore, KAHE was built as a space to study and experiment using a collective framework for knowledgeproduction throughartistic production together with the people. KAHE sees people as the first and main infrastructure in every cultural activity. Therefore, the orientation of KAHE&#x2019;s works is to develop human resources and capacities.

In 2020 KAHE initiated the &#x201c;Voicing Bajo and Bugis People of Maumere&#x201d; Project. Mediated by a collaborative artistic encounter,the goal of this project is to create an alternative public space and dialogue. At the same time, the project also seeks to facilitate Kampung Wuring as an open space for the broader community of Maumere, Sikka regency, NTT. The products of this project included art workshops with the residents, art production with residents in the form of photo books, digital comics, traditional recipe books, video documentaries), art festivals (online and offline exhibitions, pencaksilat, performances with Waria, fashion parades and hyenas). Whatmakes we&#x2019;re proud of was that we&#x2019;re able to map and document theBugis/Bajo cultural heritage in Wuring such as traditional rituals orceremony, arts, culinary arts, folklore, history, customs, and problems of the residents, to disseminate and amplify knowledge documentation products covering issues, culture, and potential for the development of Wuring .This project also has succeeded in breaking the view which considers Kampung Wuring as exclusive and brought together the residents and interests of Wuring with the relevant government institutions particularly, the Department of Tourism and Culture and the Ministry of Education and Culture in Sikka regency.</narrative>
